  "id": "example-event-driven-v1",
  "title": "Event-driven Azure integration",
  "description": "## Overview\n\nThis original synthetic example separates producers, messaging, and consumers. It is not deployed and is not a direct Microsoft Learn adaptation. Boundaries describe intended owners rather than verified access controls.\n\n## Flow\n\nAn API and producer application create commands, documents, and telemetry. Event Grid carries notifications, Service Bus carries queued work, and Event Hubs carries streams. Functions process these paths into Cosmos DB and an archive, with secrets and monitoring as supporting dependencies.\n\n## Decisions\n\nNotifications, commands, and streams have different delivery and processing requirements. Define retries, idempotency, ordering, retention, and poison-message ownership separately. The drawing does not promise exactly-once processing or atomic updates across services.\n\n## Start Editing\n\nKeep only the messaging paths your application needs. Rename event contracts and owners, define failure recovery, and identify sensitive data before connecting actual systems. Add operational controls after clarifying each consumer's responsibility.\n\n## Reference\n\nOriginal bundled Event-driven illustration, not copied from a specific Learn architecture. Consult current product documentation when turning these illustrative interactions into implementation decisions.",
  "subtitle": "Illustrative notification, command and stream processing. No live integration is implied.",
